A||||10||My7Fieros||AARRGG!!! **%$#@ BELT SQUEEKING! HELP!||||8||
Z||000000||My7Fieros||06-19-2000||02:23 AM||||Its that d*%m alt. belt. On all of my Fieros the belt squeeks for a couple of minutes at start up. Even replacing the belts and adjusting the alt. dosnt help more than a day or so. I use that belt dressing and it works for a while. Doesnt really cause any problems, just annoying...hard to look good when you start your car and it goes "SQUEEEEEEEEEK"...is this a common Fiero problem, or just mine?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Haze_Performance||06-19-2000||05:00 AM||||I changed the belts and all the squeeking stoped. that was 3 months ago...so far so good||||reg||14
Z||000002||Homicidal||06-19-2000||11:11 AM||||Nope, I got the SAME thing.  Changing the belt will only help for a bit.<P>Your glazing your belt, which means it is slipping.  Take it to a shop and tell them to tighten it correctly, or do it yourself.<P>The belt dressing is actually doing worse to the belt by weakening it's strength.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-19-2000||11:24 AM||||Another thing to look at is the pulleys. If one is bent or not "on line" with the others it will cause the same problem. I had to change my alternator twice! The first one didn't line up right. Also if the waterpump or alternator bearings are going bad and causing drag this could cause the same problem. Just some other things to check.||||reg||4
Z||000004||DXR_DAD||06-19-2000||12:15 PM||||Yes this is a known problem. About a year ago<BR>mine started this squeak thing the belt LOOKED good so I tighten it and it stoped for a day or two, so I just bought another one cleaned the pulleys and its been good to go!<P>About 3 weeks ago I had to do the water pump<BR>and I looked at the belt.........and I said just for the heck of it get a new one, so I did.<P>Homicidal your right about the belt dress!!<BR>GET new rubber and tighten correctly Make sure all bolts are tight re-check after a few days of driving.||||reg||14
Z||000005||StuGood||06-19-2000||02:52 PM||||On V6's at least, if you're not careful, that nice cast aluminum bracket for the alternator breaks pretty easily.  Especially the thin part with the big "grin" slot for the adjusting bolt.  Especially if you're mad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  Broke mine many years ago, trying to tighten the belt from underneath  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.<P>Fortunately, I was able to drive the car that way- got a replacement bracket, installed it, and tightened the belt by removing the RR tire and inner fender splash liner.  You can get to things MUCH more easily from the RR wheel well.<P>It was still a pain to try and pivot alternator with one hand to tighten the belt, using other hand to tighten the bolt.  Anyone know of any tricks to make it easier?  ||||reg||4
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||06-19-2000||03:09 PM||||StuGood, I think you got the trick, remove the r/r tire. Thats why I replaced mine when I did the water pump. Just get the a long pry bar and get in between the atl. it has to be the right lenght not too long or you won't have enough room, but once you get under the car with the wheel off you can find ways to do it. the first time I had my son help that made it easy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Linx||06-19-2000||06:51 PM||||Yknow- Ive seen this belt tightening issue addressed so many times here...and it always ends in a recommendation to buy that tool from JC Whitney.  The expander that slips between the pulleys, and lets you adjust tension with one hand.  I bought it; the tube is a little long and had to be trimmed, but it works like a charm.  $7.  No more crawling under the car with a pry bar, or removing the tire.  I wish I'd have thought of this.<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/tool.jpg"> <P>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 06-19-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 06-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||06-19-2000||11:19 PM||||That's the best-looking $7 tool I ever saw!<BR>Good thing JCW sends me a new "may be my LAST" catalog every month!||||reg||10
Z||000009||2birds||06-20-2000||12:25 AM||||That tool works wonders, but it still helps to have three hands.<P>I fought belt squeal ever since I changed my timing chain and gears.  It finally dawned on me that all the oily crap under my engine and all over my pulleys wasn't anti-freeze, but real oil.  My harmonic balancer had grooves worn in it from the original seal, and the new seal didn't match up well enough.  Fel-Pro makes a balancer sleeve kit for around $8 (Advance Auto).  New balancer seal was about the same price.  Goodyear Gatorback belts are real nice, since water tends to get on it easily, and cost around $8 from Summit.  Some 400 grit wet/dry sandpaper and about two hours (if you take your time) is all it takes to fix the oil leak, once and for all.  <P>And now, after 7 years and countless belts, no more squeal.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Blaster||06-20-2000||03:16 PM||||Ok.... I had the same problem with my 86se, but mine was from oil from the pan.  I suggest getting under the car and checking to make sure you don't have an oil pan or front cover leak.  When the oil hits the harmonic balancer it get slung all over and eventually glazes the belt.  If you do have an oil pan leak, check your front engine mount too.  When rubber engine mounts get covered in oil, then tend to absorb and get alot of play in them.  One other thing, just stick your hand down under the oil pressure sensor, if you can, and check around there and the tubing.  And make sure to clean the pulleys good, parts cleaner or something, but make sure whatever you put on you get all of it off.  And get a new belt.<P>Oh ya, and about that Belt Dressing stuff, I was told that it is made for V-Belts and not to be used on serpentine belts.  I tried it too and the squeeling stopped for oh, about 5 friggin minutes.<P>Hope it helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR>||||reg||1
